== Currently Reading

A running list. No endorsement implied -- these are just what is open in my tabs.

- Song, Dhariwal, Chen, Sutskever, /Consistency Models/, [https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.01469 arXiv:2303.01469]. Still the cleanest statement of the "map any point on the trajectory back to its origin" idea.
- Salimans and Ho, /Progressive Distillation for Fast Sampling of Diffusion Models/, [https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.00512 arXiv:2202.00512]. The halving argument is worth working through by hand once.
- Hastie, Montanari, Rosset, Tibshirani, /Surprises in High-Dimensional Ridgeless Least Squares Interpolation/, [https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08560 arXiv:1903.08560]. The paper that turned double descent from a picture into asymptotics.
- Belkin, Hsu, Ma, Mandal, /Reconciling modern machine learning practice and the bias-variance trade-off/, [https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.11118 arXiv:1812.11118].
- Messenger and Bortz, /Weak SINDy/. The weak-form idea applied to sparse system identification, and the reference I send people to first.
- Isola, Zhu, Zhou, Efros, /Image-to-Image Translation with Conditional Adversarial Networks/, [https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.07004 arXiv:1611.07004], and Zhu et al., /CycleGAN/, [https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.10593 arXiv:1703.10593]. Older, but I reread these whenever I want to remember what a loss function is for.

== A Few Things I Believe

Loosely held, and I have changed my mind on at least two of these before.

- If a method has a hyperparameter you are not tuning, your experiment is measuring the hyperparameter, not the method.
- Most "surprising" phenomena in high-dimensional statistics are surprising relative to an intuition built at $n \gg p$, and stop being surprising once you redo the calculation in the right regime.
- A proof that a method cannot work is worth several weeks of engineering time, and it is almost always cheaper to obtain.
- Numerical differentiation is a last resort. If you find yourself estimating a fourth derivative from noisy samples, back up and reformulate.
- Benchmarks measure what is easy to measure. The gap between that and what you care about is where most of the interesting work is.
